# Overview
#
# A contrastive negation denies one framing and asserts another (`This
# isn't a cache; it's a ledger`), so its first half answers a claim
# nobody made. The fix states what the thing is, and gives the contrast
# a sentence of its own only when a reader would expect the denied
# framing. The rule sits at Recommended, because a denial can also state
# a fact that the reader needs.
#
# Expression
#
# `neg` holds the negation, which is `isn't` or `is not`, their plural
# and past forms, or `it's not`, `that's not`, or `they're not` (`That's
# not a bug, it's a feature`). `then` holds the words that open the
# second half, such as `it's` and `they are`.
#
# The semicolon branch takes any words between the negation and the
# semicolon, up to six of them. The comma branch needs a word from
# `lead` after the negation. `lead` holds `about`, the minimizing words
# `just`, `only`, `merely`, and `simply`, and the tag `DET` (a
# determiner). A determiner is a word that introduces a noun, such as
# `the`, `a`, or `this`. Harper's part-of-speech tagger marks each word
# with a tag for its word class, and a pattern names a tag in capitals.
# After `it's`, the comma branch needs a determiner or `about` (`isn't
# about speed, it's about correctness`).
#
# A list picks its longest match and never backtracks, so each gap
# length is its own alternative in `semi` and `comma`. Each alternative
# ends in the punctuation, the way ProseNotOnly writes its `but` tail. A
# space in a pattern is a whitespace token, so each `*` is one word, and
# the punctuation sits glued to the last one. By default, a Weir match
# stops at a comma, so `let scope "Sentence"` lets the comma branch
# cross one. The semicolon branch needs the same scope.
#
# A sentence that splits the two halves with a period passes (`It's not
# about speed. It's about correctness.`), because a rule reads one
# sentence at a time.
#
# False Positives
#
# The guards on the comma branch keep out an `if` sentence, which takes
# the same form with a verb after `it's` (`If the file isn't found, it's
# created`). Weir has no lookbehind, so the pattern cannot look for the
# `if` before the negation. An `if` sentence with a determiner after the
# negation still counts (`If it's not a file, it's a directory`), and
# the rule accepts that misfire.
#
# Related Rules
#
# ProseSemicolon reports the semicolon of the same sentence, and
# ProseNegativeContraction reports `isn't`. `This isn't a cache; it's a
# ledger` therefore draws a finding from each of the three rules, and no
# guard separates them.
#
# ProseNotOnly reports `not just` and `not only`, and it reports `just`
# after `isn't`, `aren't`, `wasn't`, or `weren't`. A match of the comma
# branch with one of those forms (`The hook isn't just a linter, it's a
# gate`) therefore draws a finding from that rule too, and no guard
# separates the two. `only` after one of those four contractions draws
# no ProseNotOnly finding (`The hook isn't only a linter, it's a gate`).
#
# Measurement
#
# The rule hit 4 lines in the measure.py corpus, and 3 of 4 denied one
# framing before asserting another. The misfire denied a fact the reader
# needs (`the arguments are not available to the template; they are used
# for cache keys`). The `it's not` forms hit none.
expr neg [isn't, aren't, wasn't, weren't, (is not), (are not), (was not), (were not), (it's not), (that's not), (they're not)]
expr then [it's, they're, (it is), (they are), (it was), (they were)]
expr lead [DET, about, just, only, merely, simply]
expr semi [(*;), (* *;), (* * *;), (* * * *;), (* * * * *;), (* * * * * *;)]
expr comma [(*,), (* *,), (* * *,), (* * * *,), (* * * * *,)]
expr main [(@neg @semi @then), (@neg @lead @comma @then [DET, about])]
let message "Contrastive negation. State what it is, and give the contrast its own sentence only if a reader expects it."
let description "Flags a clause that denies one framing before asserting another, such as `this isn't a cache; it's a ledger`."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
let scope "Sentence"
allows "If the file isn't found, it's created."
allows "The value isn't a number, so the parser rejects it."
allows "The cache is a ledger of every decision the scheduler makes."
